00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:42.280
Another thing is that
we've integrated our IoT

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.470
with the rest of
our CRM platform,

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:47.380
so you can rich
your IoT with all

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.810
of the data from Service
Cloud and Sales Cloud

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:53.430
and any of the other information
that you have in CRM.

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:56.290
And finally, proactive
customer engagement.

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.540
I mean, that's what we're
really talking about.

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.100
We don't want to be
reactive nowadays

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.599
we don't want to think, oh, I
wonder what my customer needs.

00:13:02.599 --> 00:13:04.690
We want to proactively
engage with them

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:08.420
through this IoT platform.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.710
So let's talk a little
bit about how we work

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:14.350
and how we complement the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:17.560
So basically as we said
before, we get the device

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:19.240
from these things--

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:21.050
the data from these things.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:24.040
And we put it into the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.530
And then we sent it
the Salesforce IoT.

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:29.300
And what we do there
is we create a profile.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:34.270
And that profile is IoT data
as well as contextual data

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.400
that we get from Salesforce.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:39.370
And then we create what we
call an orchestration, which

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:41.230
is basically a bunch
of business rules

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.960
that we apply to
these profiles, and we

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:47.442
trigger again some sort of
proactive customer engagement.

00:13:47.442 --> 00:13:49.400
That could be in Sales
Cloud, or Service Cloud,

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:50.680
or Marketing Cloud.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.990
And one of the other things
that you need to consider

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.610
is what other data
might you need.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:56.364
What other data might
want to pull in?

00:13:56.364 --> 00:13:58.280
So you might want to
pull in third-party data,

00:13:58.280 --> 00:13:59.800
for example, weather data.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.860
You might want to pull
in Google Maps data.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.510
And just to give a quick
example of how this might work,

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.110
let's say it's a very warm day.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:10.954
And you have a Coca-Cola
cooler in your office,

00:14:10.954 --> 00:14:12.370
and you want to
get to your office

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:14.020
and get a nice cold Coke out.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:15.700
But you know since
it's really warm out,

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:17.980
there's probably not going
to be a lot of Coke left

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:19.360
in there when you get there.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:21.540
Well, Coca-Cola
also knows that it's

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:25.330
a warm day out because
they have an IoT platform

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.490
and they're saying,
oh, OK, you know what?

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:29.860
We've got our third-party
data from the Weather Service.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:32.260
We know it's going to
be really warm out,

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:33.910
and we can look
inside a Salesforce

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:36.370
and we can see where
our coolers are.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.980
And we can see that we
have a field service

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:43.030
guy who can actually go out
and actually dispatch some more

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:45.604
Coke to-- proactively--
to that location

00:14:45.604 --> 00:14:47.020
so that when you
get to the office

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:48.760
you can have your
nice cold Coke.

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:52.540
That's an example
of how IoT can work.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.580
So hopefully if you
have any other questions

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:56.590
around Salesforce IoT,
you'll stick around.

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:58.210
You can ask us afterwards.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:01.600
I'd now like to introduce my
colleague Aubrey Wing, who's

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.300
actually going to talk about
and provide a demo on how

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:06.880
this is really going to work
as well with the refrigerator.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:07.727
Thank you.

00:15:07.727 --> 00:15:10.498
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:10.498 --> 00:15:11.623
AUBREY WING: Hi, everybody.

00:15:11.623 --> 00:15:18.520
If we could go ahead and
switch over to the demo screen.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:20.750
Of course, since it's a
demo, computer went to sleep,

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:23.370
so Roberto, I'm going to have
to ask you to log back in.

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.560
While we're doing that,
what I wanted to do was just

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:28.771
give you a high-level
overview of the use case

00:15:28.771 --> 00:15:30.270
that we're going
to see in the demo.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:32.450
And we're going to see
the demo in two parts.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:34.572
The first half is
going to be Google.

00:15:34.572 --> 00:15:36.280
The second half is
going to be Salesforce

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.458
and really watching the
two pieces work together.

00:15:38.458 --> 00:15:41.380
But the key thing that you're
hearing in this session today

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:44.580
is that you want to think
about your use case.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:47.850
You want to go ahead and
make sure that you're

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.860
driving business value.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.950
So ultimately what we're
going to take a look at

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:59.220
is how you can apply IoT
to answer some really

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.440
exciting business questions.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:02.940
And so what I wanted
to do was start

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.224
by introducing our use
case, and then we'll

00:16:05.224 --> 00:16:06.640
go into the different
pieces here.

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:11.380
It is a demo, you guys.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:13.060
You can see that
this is in fact live.

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:21.340
So our use case here is
based upon logistics.

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:25.670
It's a pharmaceutical use case
where really the underlying

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:29.000
driver is to go ahead and
ensure product quality.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.384
And product quality comes
across three primary dimensions.

00:16:32.384 --> 00:16:33.800
First and foremost,
I want to make

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:36.440
sure I have the right
level stock in the places

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.180
that it's needed the most.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.640
So think of this as do
I have the right stock

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:44.780
level in New York or
London or San Francisco.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:48.020
The second aspect of
this are the assets

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:53.510
that I own to make sure that
the product is within spec.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.690
So think of this as all of
the refrigerated box cars

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:59.120
or all of the
airplanes that we use

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:03.740
to make sure that we're moving
product from place to place.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:07.579
And the third is, especially in
high-value products or anything

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:09.710
that has a determined
spec, that we

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:11.960
want to go ahead
and say that we're

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:15.050
keeping that as it should be.

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:17.470
So in this case, we're
monitoring environmentals--

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.480
the right temperature, the right
humidity, the right pressure,

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:21.770
whatever it may be.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.899
So put it simply, is my
product the right place,

00:17:23.899 --> 00:17:26.000
is it the right
quality, am I keeping

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:28.069
track of cold chain custody?

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:31.070
Bunch of different use cases
all driving towards a singular

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.444
business experience.

00:17:32.444 --> 00:17:33.860
Now historically
what has happened

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:38.290
is that's three
separate logic sets.

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:42.090
You'll have to join
them and then test them.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:44.030
So that's months and
months and months.

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:45.830
And with Google
and Salesforce, we

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.020
can go ahead and get
something like that working

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:50.780
and the matter of
weeks not months.

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:52.220
And, in fact the
business analysts

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:53.840
can go ahead and build that.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:56.360
So just to highlight real
quick, what we're looking at

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:57.924
is Salesforce IoT here.

00:17:57.924 --> 00:17:59.840
But then we're going to
work backwards and see

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.830
how these two work together.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:04.970
So I mentioned
earlier, if you look

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:06.590
in the upper
left-hand corner, you

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:10.650
see a warning around
geolocation or geoboundary.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:12.200
The second we're
going to see here

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:14.570
is our evaluation
of the equipment.

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:17.300
Is in fact the equipment--
is the door jammed?

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:18.730
Is it operating?

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:20.450
Is it pulling high voltage?

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:22.350
And the last is
actually temperature.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.520
And to represent this, this
wouldn't be an IoT demo

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.830
if we didn't in fact
have hardware here

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:29.810
so you can see this run live.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.330
We have a refrigerator
representing the cold chain.

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.520
We have a sensor
pack in here that's

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.010
going to give us live readings.

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:38.427
And so to best
highlight that, I'm

00:18:38.427 --> 00:18:39.968
actually going to
turn the stage back

00:18:39.968 --> 00:18:42.276
to Roberto, who will take
you through the Google site.

00:18:42.276 --> 00:18:43.400
ROBERTO BARBERO: Thank you.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.680
Can we just go back for
a second to these lights?

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:51.030
Because I would like to show
you what is the architecture

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:53.320
that we are seeing here.

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:57.520
So we have-- in fact, being
an IoT demos, what we said,

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:00.340
out of reach inside there is--

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:01.290
let me show you.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:08.070
We have our sensors running
here, happily, as you can see.

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:08.860
It's blinking.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:09.390
It's live.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:12.540
I have temperature,
humidity, pressure.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:16.325
I have many, many, many sensors
embedded here on this fridge.

00:19:16.325 --> 00:19:20.360
So, yes, make sure that the
chain is working correctly.

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:24.510
This Raspberry-- these sensors
are sitting actually using

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:25.470
the Wi-Fi--

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:29.340
fingers crossed-- the
data to the cloud.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:32.380
Of course, we have at
the very front IoT Core.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:34.920
IoT Core is giving
me the security.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.140
It's giving me this--

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.010
the device management,
it's giving me

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.940
this capability to bring
these things to the cloud.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:43.770
IoT Core is glued to Pub/Sub.

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:45.320
Pub/Sub is our queuing system.

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:48.630
It's kind of like the way that
I can get information and make

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.460
sure that this
telemetry data is not

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:54.870
lost if I have silently a
burst of information coming in.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:58.200
And then Pub/Sub is also
the same time the way

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:02.390
that I can plug in the different
pieces of the solution that

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.380
will give you a
Google Cloud platform.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:08.190
If I go southbound, for
example, we have Dataflow.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.590
Probably I will have
different sensors

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:14.880
from the truck that is
transporting these vaccines.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:18.061
And I have information about
what it is from the GPS,

00:20:18.061 --> 00:20:20.310
and I have information from
the temperature and so on.

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:23.640
I have to make sure that
all that information is

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:26.020
put together in real time.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:28.260
And this is what Dataflow
is going to help you to do.

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:30.300
In real time, you can
transform the data.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:33.060
You can massage the data to
make sure it's in the format

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:36.390
and complement that data to make
sure that you have everything

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:37.410
that you need.

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:40.050
And then I'm going to
store the data somewhere.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:43.410
Here in this example we
are using BigQuery, our--

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:46.470
you heard a lot about BigQuery
right now, our data warehouse

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:48.850
solution, but depending
on your use case,

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:52.470
we can suggest which is the
best storage solution for you.

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:55.680
From there, what I am doing
is just gathering this data

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:02.220
so I can show you in real
time how it is showing.

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:05.920
If I go northbound, I
have Cloud functions,

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.139
which is just a small piece
of code that is actually--

00:21:09.139 --> 00:21:10.680
it's actually
triggered by something,

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.537
in this case, the telemetry
that comes from the sensors.

00:21:13.537 --> 00:21:15.120
And that is going
to make the solution

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:17.110
and inject that data
into Salesforce.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.410
And Aubrey will show you
a bit more about that.

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.290
So let me just go
back to the demo, now

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:25.800
that you have seen
the main architecture,

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.800
just to make sure that you
see that this is actually

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.040
in real time.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:33.030
This is the old information
that is coming in.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:34.530
And for those of
you that would like

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:37.990
to see a bit more under the
hood what this is about,

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:40.620
this is the Google
Cloud Platform console.

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:43.030
And I have here, we say it--

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:46.260
IoT Core is the first step.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:49.410
IoT Core, I have
different registries here.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:53.370
I have the registry, which is
just how I group the devices.

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:58.320
And as you will see, this
registry has some devices.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:01.260
In this case, I have
three fridges here.

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:03.750
I have the one that is
in San Francisco that

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:05.780
is communicating.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:09.640
And I can see all the
telemetry data when it happens.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.590
If I dig down a little bit,
I can show you, for example,

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:14.660
how I connect IoT Core.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:17.240
I said-- I talked about
security-- a device should not

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:18.198
have-- is not a person.

00:22:18.198 --> 00:22:20.630
It shouldn't have a user
name and a password.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:23.180
For us, a device will have
its own private key that

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:24.920
is only known by the device.

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:29.266
I will use that private key to
sign tokens to talk with us.

00:22:29.266 --> 00:22:32.740
IoT caller will then
take the path publicly,

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:35.300
just matches the token
that the service has--

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.070
their device has sent to us.

00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:41.760
So I'm sure that the device
is exactly who it says it is.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:43.640
And from now on,
that information,

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:47.630
the telemetry data, is coming
encrypted into IoT Core.

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:51.290
The next step from
IoT Core is Pub/Sub.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:53.030
That is just a bit boring.

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:56.360
I can show you here that
this registry is sending me

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:59.660
telemetry data to
this Pub/Sub topic--

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:00.980
this queuing system.

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:04.670
And if you remember, the
next step is Dataflow.

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:08.960
What I am doing this real-time
transformation of the data.

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:12.080
I have one here that is doing
something very, very simple

00:23:12.080 --> 00:23:13.340
for the demo purposes of this.

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:17.351
It's just reading the telemetry
data that comes from Pub/Sub.

00:23:17.351 --> 00:23:19.100
The Raspberry is
actually sending the data

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:20.130
in JSON format.

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.790
And I want the data to
be injected in BigQuery,

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:25.460
so I am transforming
that into BigQuery format

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:29.190
and then injecting
it into BigQuery.

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:32.520
And the last step
is store the data.

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:37.350
So here I have my Datastore
in BigQuery with all the data

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:38.580
from the fridge.

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:40.830
And I'm going to actually
just query the table so you

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:42.630
see that this is coming live.

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:45.480
So I want to see everything
from this data set,

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:50.941
and I'm going to order
it by the timestamp.

00:23:50.941 --> 00:23:53.190
For those of you that use
Salesforce and [INAUDIBLE],,

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:55.750
you know where this
is coming from.

00:23:55.750 --> 00:24:04.470
Descending, I run the query, and
you will see that at 20:59 UTC,

00:24:04.470 --> 00:24:08.580
I am receiving telemetry
data that actually 20.62--

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:13.620
22.63 corresponds to the live
data I'm getting for there.

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:15.720
So this data-- you remember
this is a southbound.

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:19.730
I am getting these data, and I
am going to analyze that data.

00:24:19.730 --> 00:24:22.530
I can do something
meaningful with it.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:26.280
The last piece-- if you
remember, if I go northbound--

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:28.380
I have a Cloud
function that has been

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:29.580
triggered every single time.

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:31.785
So every three
seconds when this--

00:24:31.785 --> 00:24:35.800
the Raspberry is sending me
data every three seconds,

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:38.730
I have this function
that is kicking in,

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:43.490
and I can show you
that what it's doing

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:46.310
is actually connecting
to Salesforce.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:49.040
Everything is finishing
with a status OK.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:52.630
So, Aubrey, if you want to show
us what happens after this.

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:56.630
AUBREY WING: Absolutely.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:01.130
Great.

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:03.420
Thank you, Roberto.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:07.590
I think that the best thing to
think about from the next step

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:12.690
is that as the northbound
lanes start sending data,

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:14.580
this is our
bi-directional ability

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:17.700
to communicate back
and forth with GCP.

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:19.320
You're seeing the
information here

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:23.190
directly from our fridge
and a Raspberry Pi.

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:25.950
I gave you a quick
view just a minute ago

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:28.340
on what it looks like
in terms of Salesforce.

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:31.470
Again, in Salesforce,
our mission

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:34.090
is to make IoT
really accessible.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:36.870
We're taking, in this
case, a state machine,

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:41.070
which can be a very powerful
and sophisticated thing that we

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:46.470
can offer to big business
users to make it accessible

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:48.910
and solve real-world
business problems.

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:50.900
So I mentioned earlier,
the different states

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:52.920
as we go through from
normal to warning

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:57.450
across the different use
cases all the way to fault.

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:00.910
What happens at that point is
that we're able to take action.

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:03.690
So you think about Google
as anomaly detection,

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:06.765
a lot of data, the ability to
go ahead and manage security,

00:26:06.765 --> 00:26:09.270
to go ahead and manage
data moving back and forth,

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:10.717
provide data services.

00:26:10.717 --> 00:26:12.300
With Salesforce,
we'll allow you to do

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:16.210
is take the anomaly detection
and turn that into response.

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:17.740
The response is
the last mile where

00:26:17.740 --> 00:26:20.007
we have that customer
behind the scenes.

00:26:20.007 --> 00:26:21.840
So what I'm going to
do here is I'm actually

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.720
going to reach over and
change one of the parameters.

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:26.910
You'll see it change on
both the Google as well

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:29.640
as the Salesforce site.

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:31.315
So if we go ahead
and take a look

00:26:31.315 --> 00:26:32.440
at the hardware real quick.

00:26:42.740 --> 00:26:45.930
For the folks in the front
row here, but certainly

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:47.770
we can go ahead and
show you later on,

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:49.912
I'm going to increase one
of the environmentals--

00:26:49.912 --> 00:26:51.120
the temperature and humidity.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:55.190
I'm going to be very fancy
here just to do this manually.

00:26:59.330 --> 00:27:00.465
Hold on a second.

00:27:00.465 --> 00:27:01.590
We'll keep working on this.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:08.490
And what you're going to see,
as soon as it gets the reading,

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:10.690
is that we're going to go
ahead and change states.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:12.160
There it is.

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:15.370
So we now have a
temperature warning.

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:15.880
Keep going.

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:18.002
ROBERTO BARBERO: Keep
blowing, keep blowing!

00:27:18.002 --> 00:27:19.960
AUBREY WING: I feel like
I'm blowing up balloon

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:21.160
animals at a birthday party.

00:27:27.286 --> 00:27:28.270
I think that'll do it.

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:35.020
And the reason why we do
this, we introduce states,

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:37.090
is that with IoT, you're
going to continue-- get

00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:40.810
a continuous flow of data where
you do not want to necessarily

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:41.950
respond to the first event.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:42.580
There we go.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:43.570
All right.

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:44.290
We have our case.

00:27:48.637 --> 00:27:50.970
We don't necessarily want to
respond to the first event.

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:53.180
So let's say, for
example, my business logic

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:55.850
was around stimulus response,
which is a really common way

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:56.714
to look at this.

00:27:56.714 --> 00:27:57.380
I get a reading.

00:27:57.380 --> 00:27:58.070
I open a case.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.150
I get a reading.

00:27:59.150 --> 00:28:02.020
I actually send-- let's
say a field dispatch,

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:04.800
but I'm getting a reading at
the rate of once per second.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:07.910
So I'm going to get 60 cases or
60 dispatch notices in a minute

00:28:07.910 --> 00:28:10.260
if I'm not more
sophisticated with this.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:13.610
So what we're doing here,
as Lisa mentioned earlier,

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:16.010
is that we're contextualizing.

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.652
You have our fridge data coming
in off the left-hand side.

00:28:18.652 --> 00:28:21.110
It's an example of the streaming
live event data that we're

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:23.600
getting from GCP.

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:27.250
We're adding the asset data,
really important elements.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:28.760
When was this in service?

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:30.890
Does it have a warranty?

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:32.090
What should we be doing?

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:38.750
So what I'm going
to show you here

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:41.570
is all of the logic that sits
behind the scenes of the states

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:42.322
that you just saw.

00:28:42.322 --> 00:28:44.030
Now a little bit of
an eye chart-- again,

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:45.949
happy to go over this
with you in more detail

00:28:45.949 --> 00:28:47.490
either after the
session or I'm going

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:50.730
to encourage you to go to either
the GCP IoT or the Salesforce

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:51.860
booths downstairs.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.510
Love to dig into this stuff.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:56.900
But bottom line is, you can,
without having to program,

00:28:56.900 --> 00:29:00.120
get access to very
sophisticated functionality.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.120
We can go back and say,
what are our targets?

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:03.800
How long do you want to wait?

00:29:03.800 --> 00:29:08.960
One of the keys here is
that when I had a fault,

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:11.000
I'm going to check
for a warranty.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.070
And the reason why I
do that is because I'm

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:15.110
going to behave
different depending

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:17.960
on the contextual information
contained within Salesforce.

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:22.440
The customer has a warranty,
I open a case, I dispatch.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:25.040
They are automatically
entitled to service rights

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:27.080
if they don't have a warranty.

00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:29.150
We open an opportunity
to sell them a warranty

00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:32.042
or perhaps sell them
the replacement parts.

00:29:32.042 --> 00:29:33.500
If they're in a
marketing campaign,

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:35.420
perhaps we're going to
alter the marketing.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:38.600
One event will change
the entire relationship

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:40.190
a customer can
have with the brand

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:42.450
across multiple functions.

00:29:42.450 --> 00:29:44.780
So I do want to highlight
that as one of the key things

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:48.150
that we bring to the table.

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:49.800
What does a case look like?

00:29:49.800 --> 00:29:51.510
In this case, this is the asset.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:55.220
Salesforce IoT requested
a response independent.

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:58.020
You can start automating
these key business processes

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:02.250
and determining what your ideal
customer experience should be.

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:08.700
And ultimately, we're able to
see IoT information directly

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.770
inside CRM or Salesforce.

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:12.980
Now if you go and you look
at the entire architecture

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:14.220
while you're all here--

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:15.640
why are we here?

00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:18.950
The physical things in
IoT have a digital voice.

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:23.370
That digital voice is what your
customers want to tell you.

00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:27.630
Use the data to listen to
the customers, use sales--

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:30.810
use GCP to process
and gain insight to be

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:34.140
able to manage the
identity, use Salesforce

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:37.660
to be able to connect to
your customer directly.

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:40.900
That being said, I believe
we've resolved our problem.

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:41.680
There it is.

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:42.681
So we're back to normal.

00:30:42.681 --> 00:30:44.596
I think because I threw
it back in the fridge,

00:30:44.596 --> 00:30:46.690
but if you guys want to
manually change this,

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:48.700
you're absolutely welcome to.

00:30:48.700 --> 00:30:54.975
And again, as we think about the
possibilities of the use case,

00:30:54.975 --> 00:30:56.830
this is where I
get very excited.

00:30:56.830 --> 00:31:00.590
The key takeaway here is that
IoT architecture improving

00:31:00.590 --> 00:31:03.460
